WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.234
-Last time you were here --
Since the last time

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:04.634
you were here,
you were a newlywed.

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:05.801
You were having your first baby.

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:08.334
Now you're a dad of three girls.
-I am.

00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:09.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Come on, bud.

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:10.901
-Yeah, I know.

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:12.901
Well, First of all, Jimmy,

00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:14.801
thanks for taking
six years to invite me back.

00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:16.067
I appreciate that, yeah.

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.334
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
No problem, buddy. No problem.

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:20.200
-Three girls -- 5, 4, and 1.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.167
-Oh, my God.
What is typical day like?

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.767
-Yeah, "Whoo." Pray for me.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.567
-It's just packed, jam-packed.
-You know what?

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:30.100
It's -- I don't want to say --
Let me be careful here.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.234
Controlled chaos is
the best way to put it.

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:34.434
-It's just
always something happening.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.834
-It's, you know,
school pickups, drop-offs.

00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:41.267
It's -- They do my nails,
my toenails, makeup, lipstick.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:44.067
And, you know,
it's the greatest thing

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.434
I've ever experienced, though.

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:48.567
I mean, my girls are --
They are the absolute best.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:51.200
And, you know, it's --
At least I --

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.734
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:57.300
Don't clap yet, because I heard
around 13 years of age

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.501
is when they don't
like you very much.

00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:01.000
-No, yeah -- No, that's a rumor.
That's a rumor.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.367
-I'm preparing for that.
-Come on.

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:05.133
They're going to love you.
Come on. You're a great dad.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.067
Aren't you psyched
that you don't have long hair?

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.868
They would probably be
braiding it all day.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.667
-Yeah, they tried
when they were younger, yes,

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.701
but this --
you know, it's by choice.

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:16.067
-Yeah, of course it is. Yeah.
Of course it is.

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:19.300
-I used to have flowing locks,
like you, not too long ago.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.868
-Are they into baseball or
softball or anything

00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:23.067
or too little?

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:24.434
-I think they're
a little too young.

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:26.667
You know, they don't really
know much about my career.

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.834
They came to Yankee Stadium for
the first time a few months ago,

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.100
were on the field.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.100
So they understand
that I played for the Yankees.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.434
But, you know, I think sports
for kids is unbelievable.

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.334
Teach you a lot of life lessons.
-Of course.

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:39.334
-You know,
teaches you shared goals,

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:42.033
you know, teamwork,
work ethic, dedication.

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:45.934
And my wife used to play tennis,
so we put our oldest

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:48.334
in tennis lessons
when she was about 4 years old.

00:01:48.334 --> 00:01:50.200
So she's taking
the tennis lessons.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.601
And, look, I was so proud
because she actually got to be

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:55.968
pretty good for the 15 minutes
that she would focus,

00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:57.801
and then it's going
and drawing in the clay.

00:01:57.801 --> 00:01:59.100
You know, so, in my head,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.367
I'm not one of those parents
that scream at anyone,

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:02.868
but I'm like, you know,
"Listen, focus.

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:04.367
You know,
you're embarrassing us."

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:07.801
So, one day,
we're taking her to practice,

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:09.667
and I say, "You know, Bella,

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:11.234
are you ready to go to
tennis practice?"

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:12.334
She said, "I don't want to go."

00:02:12.334 --> 00:02:13.634
I was like,
"Alright, that's fine."

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:15.133
I said, "We'll find
something else for you to do.

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:16.934
But do you mind sharing why you

00:02:16.934 --> 00:02:18.300
don't want to go to
tennis practice?"

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.767
She said, "Dad,
I already know how to play."

00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:23.100
So she is not
lacking in confidence

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.434
when it comes to sports.

00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:25.467
-I love that.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:26.467
She's got the confidence
right there.

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:28.601
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah, I love it.

00:02:28.601 --> 00:02:31.234
We had Aaron Judge
here recently on the show,

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:35.767
and he is now named
the new captain --

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:37.434
Yankee captain
since your retirement.

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:38.734
And he said
he got to talk to you

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:42.133
and you were very nice to him
and very funny with him.

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:44.400
Do you remember when you got
the call to be captain?

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.200
-I do.
I thought I was in trouble.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.133
You know, this is back in 2003.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:50.634
There was this false narrative
going around town

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:52.667
that I was
this big party animal,

00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:54.834
and in -- I hear one "whoo!"

00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:56.167
-Yeah.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:59.367
-So, I was injured
at the beginning of the season,

00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:01.934
and, you know, me and the boss,
Mr. Steinbrenner,

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:05.934
got into it a little bit over
this so-called "party animal"

00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:07.200
that I was being portrayed as.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.434
So I was hurt.

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:09.868
I missed the first
couple months of the season.

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.234
I get back to New York.

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:13.033
I take all my teammates --
I have them over for dinner.

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:14.167
We all go out to a club.

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:16.934
Now, granted,
we have the next day off.

00:03:16.934 --> 00:03:18.400
Next day in the paper,
headlines --

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.767
"It's 3:00, 4:00 a.m.,

00:03:19.767 --> 00:03:22.033
and Derek's
out on the town again."

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:23.834
I get a call
from our PR director.

00:03:23.834 --> 00:03:25.968
He says, "The boss
wants to talk to you."

00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:27.667
And he named me captain.

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:29.267
I think he was literally like,

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:31.434
"This guy just doesn't care
what I say,

00:03:31.434 --> 00:03:33.767
so we might as well
just name him captain."

00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:34.801
-You thought you were
in trouble?

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:36.033
-I thought I was
in trouble, yes.

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:39.000
-I went out with you to a club
a couple times.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.868
-Thanks for ruining
the false narrative

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:43.534
that I just tried saying.

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:46.567
-No, you would never --
-We went one time, one time.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:47.901
That was church. What'd we do?

00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:50.868
-We went to -- We went out --
I remember this

00:03:50.868 --> 00:03:52.334
because I was at "SNL."

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:54.667
I was on "Saturday Night Live."
And that's when I first met you.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.834
And we went out to a club.
I'd never really been to a club.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.100
-Oh, here we go.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.567
Jimmy's never been --
Yeah, okay.

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:00.868
We'll let him go.

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.834
This is another
"True Confession" right here.

00:04:02.834 --> 00:04:05.567
-No. We were at this table.
And I had never sat at a table.

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:07.334
They had table --
bottle service or whatever.

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:09.434
And I go -- I didn't know
what any of this was, right?

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.267
So, I'm sitting there.
I was there for like 20 minutes.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:12.901
We were out before that,
I think, even.

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:15.033
And I go -- And then
you went to the bathroom,

00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:16.701
and your friends were all there
and everybody.

00:04:16.701 --> 00:04:18.868
You had like 10 people with you.

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:19.934
And I go, "I'm going to split."

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:21.067
I was going to
Irish goodbye, you know,

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:22.200
and I'll text you later.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.467
I go, "I'll call you later."

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.934
So, I go,
"I'll take care of this bill,"

00:04:25.934 --> 00:04:29.367
and everyone went like, "No!"

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:30.567
It was like slow motion.

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:33.100
Like, "You make
'Saturday Night Live' money,

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.634
not Yankee money."

00:04:34.634 --> 00:04:37.501
I was like, "Let me give you
my credit card."

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:39.100
And then I get the bill.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.934
And I was there
20 minutes with you?

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:43.434
And the bill was like $2,000.

00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:45.300
And I almost started crying

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.767
because it was like
a month's rent for me.

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:49.934
I'm like, "Did we break
one of the tables?

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:51.234
What the heck happened?"

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:53.067
And then I got to tip
on top of that.

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:54.501
And I was just like --
I don't think

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:55.834
I ever went out with you
ever again.

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:57.834
-Yeah, I don't recall.

00:04:57.834 --> 00:04:59.334
You know, one of
the first things that goes

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:01.634
when you get older
is your memory,

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.901
so I don't know if you really --
I don't know if that's true.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:06.634
I really don't know
if it's true.

00:05:06.634 --> 00:05:07.634
It sounds good, though.
-It sounds good.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.033
It's a good story.

00:05:09.033 --> 00:05:12.000
Do you remember, one night,
we went out to another bar?

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.567
-Yeah, another night, right?
Yeah, another night.

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:17.000
I don't. I don't remember.
-You've got remember this one.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.000
This was a good one.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.267
This one, you might have
went home early,

00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:20.601
but I don't remember.

00:05:20.601 --> 00:05:23.434
But there's a guy -- I don't
even remember his name.

00:05:23.434 --> 00:05:25.601
I won't say his name
if I do remember his name.

00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:29.467
But he was a Yankee,
and he got so wasted.

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.300
I mean, he was drunk
and he gets up,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.601
and we were at the same table.

00:05:32.601 --> 00:05:33.968
And he gets up and he's like,

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.000
"I'm gonna go to the bathroom"
or something like that.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.133
And he goes up to this door,

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:37.534
and we're all like,
"No, no, wait, dude.

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:38.601
John --" or whatever
his name was.

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:40.033
And he goes, "Whatever, man."

00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:41.934
And he opened the door, and it
wasn't the door to the bathroom.

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:43.567
It was the emergency exit.
-And he fell out.

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:45.901
Yeah, and fell out.
That is a true story.

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:47.367
-He fell out.
-That is a true story.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:51.968
-And I remember seeing his shoes
and the doors are closed,

00:05:51.968 --> 00:05:55.534
and none of us
got up to help him.

00:05:55.534 --> 00:06:00.167
-Just to clarify, he wasn't
with us before we went out.

00:06:00.167 --> 00:06:01.467
He met us there.
-That is true.

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:02.734
Oh, yeah, that's true.
-He met us there.

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:04.200
-No, he did meet us there.
-He met us there, yes.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.367
-But I didn't see the guy
ever again in my life.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.667
I didn't know
what happened to him.

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:11.701
-I never saw him again either.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:13.968
-Oh, my gosh.
We had some good times.

00:06:13.968 --> 00:06:16.267
I want to talk about
the Turn 2 Foundation,

00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:19.467
because, man, you do
so much good stuff for charity.

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.501
I think you've raised
over $34 million with Turn 2.

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:22.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:06:22.834 --> 00:06:25.400
Which is amazing, what you do.
-Thank you very much.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.133
-These young people.

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:29.667
You're having the Derek Jeter
Celebrity Invitational

00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:32.267
golf tournament in a few months.

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:33.868
You're obviously
the one running it.

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.834
And I just find
it very interesting --

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:38.200
you've never won
your own tournament.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.501
[ Laughter ]
-Why is that funny?

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:42.100
-It's no big deal.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.634
I just think it's funny,
because someone has to win

00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:46.067
the tournament when you play.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:48.067
-I think it's two things.

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:49.934
One, if you win
your own tournament,

00:06:49.934 --> 00:06:51.334
people think it's rigged.

00:06:51.334 --> 00:06:54.067
So, you want people to come back
and continue to spend money

00:06:54.067 --> 00:06:55.501
and hope they have
a chance to win.

00:06:55.501 --> 00:06:58.501
So you can't just go out
and win your own tournament.

00:06:58.501 --> 00:07:00.901
And, two,
I'm not very good at golf.

00:07:00.901 --> 00:07:03.634
I mean, I -- You can relate.

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:06.801
-What?
-You can relate.

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.534
-You know what's fun?
-We go way back.

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:11.300
There's no --
He'll agree to this.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.567
-You know what's funny, though?

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:14.534
I mean, I don't even recall,
I mean, the past winners

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.834
of your tournament
and stuff like that, but...

00:07:16.834 --> 00:07:19.434
-Like I said, it was rigged.
You won one year.

00:07:19.434 --> 00:07:21.834
You won one year.
So we've got to have Jimmy back.

00:07:21.834 --> 00:07:24.701
-How does it feel
to sit next to a champion?

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:27.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.968 --> 00:07:31.267
Did that drive you --
I remember you were so mad.

00:07:31.267 --> 00:07:32.567
You were smiling
because you had to take

00:07:32.567 --> 00:07:34.000
a photo with the winners.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.467
And it's me,
and you're going like,

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:37.767
"I can't believe
I got to do this."

00:07:37.767 --> 00:07:39.767
And I have the photo somewhere.
-That's a true story.

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:42.067
But, you know,
your handicaps play into golf,

00:07:42.067 --> 00:07:44.934
so the worse you are,
it actually helps out your team.

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:46.834
-Yeah, it really did.
-Yeah.

00:07:46.834 --> 00:07:50.634
Was that the time my mom called?

00:07:50.634 --> 00:07:54.067
-Yeah, it was the time --
He overslept the next day.

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:55.968
He was missing his flight
to go home.

00:07:55.968 --> 00:07:58.234
His mom actually was calling

00:07:58.234 --> 00:08:00.767
all of our staff
trying to track down Jimmy.

00:08:00.767 --> 00:08:02.067
That is a true story.

00:08:02.067 --> 00:08:04.968
-There's other things
I want to talk to you about.

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:07.300
I want to talk to you about
your athletic wear.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.067
This is Greatness Wins.
-Yes.

00:08:09.067 --> 00:08:10.834
-And, dude,
congratulations on this.

00:08:10.834 --> 00:08:12.467
This is cool that you get
to do stuff like this.

00:08:12.467 --> 00:08:13.634
And, like, of course.

00:08:13.634 --> 00:08:15.367
You're one of the
best athletes in the world.

00:08:15.367 --> 00:08:17.434
You're the greatest athlete
I've ever seen play

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:19.400
in the history
of any sport ever.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.701
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Oh, really?

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:24.000
-I'm honored to get to know you,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.300
but just to watch --
You're unbelievable.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.901
-Well, I appreciate it.

00:08:27.901 --> 00:08:32.033
He made a -- You know, he made
a movie with the Boston Red Sox,

00:08:32.033 --> 00:08:33.334
so now he tries
to make up for that.

00:08:33.334 --> 00:08:34.801
-Thank you very much.
Thank you very much.

00:08:34.801 --> 00:08:38.634
-He tries to make up for that.
-I deserve an Oscar for that.

00:08:38.634 --> 00:08:42.334
-Yeah, you do.
Are you a Boston fan?

00:08:42.334 --> 00:08:44.701
-Yeah.

00:08:44.701 --> 00:08:46.801
I'm a Yankee fan,
but at the time,

00:08:46.801 --> 00:08:50.234
I was an actor
and I was playing a role.

00:08:50.234 --> 00:08:51.701
I mean, you know what
acting is, right?

00:08:51.701 --> 00:08:53.601
You know...

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:56.868
-I remember meeting Spike Lee
my first year.

00:08:56.868 --> 00:08:59.634
And I'm a big fan of Spike.
I'd never met him before.

00:08:59.634 --> 00:09:01.334
He came to a game, and I said,
"Hey, Spike, man,

00:09:01.334 --> 00:09:03.167
when are you going to let me
act in one of your movies?"

00:09:03.167 --> 00:09:06.567
He says, "Hey, why don't you act
like being a short stop today."

00:09:06.567 --> 00:09:08.400
[ Laughter ]

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:09.467
[ Cymbal crash ]
Yeah.

00:09:09.467 --> 00:09:10.767
-Come on. That's awesome.
Come on.

00:09:10.767 --> 00:09:13.400
-It's a true story. It is true.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.400
-On Monday, it was announced,

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.133
the cover athlete for
"MLB The Show 23."

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:20.300
It's someone
that you know very well.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:21.367
-Yep.

00:09:21.367 --> 00:09:23.601
-This is Miami Marlins
center fielder

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:25.267
Jazz Chisholm right there.

00:09:25.267 --> 00:09:28.801
You know -- You're the one
that helped get Jazz

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:30.234
on the Marlins, is that correct?

00:09:30.234 --> 00:09:31.767
-I did, yes. I did.

00:09:31.767 --> 00:09:33.801
You know, the great thing about
Jazz is, he's from the Bahamas,

00:09:33.801 --> 00:09:36.501
and baseball is
such an international sport.

00:09:36.501 --> 00:09:38.100
You love to see this,
because, you know,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.968
he's setting an example for
young men and women

00:09:41.968 --> 00:09:43.534
growing up in the Bahamas,
knowing that

00:09:43.534 --> 00:09:45.601
they have an opportunity
to make it to, you know,

00:09:45.601 --> 00:09:46.701
the top of their profession.

00:09:46.701 --> 00:09:48.934
So I couldn't be prouder of him.

00:09:48.934 --> 00:09:50.167
-It's a big deal
to be on the cover.

00:09:50.167 --> 00:09:51.767
-Oh, yeah. Yes.

00:09:51.767 --> 00:09:54.934
-But there's also a collector's
edition of "MLB The Show"

00:09:54.934 --> 00:09:58.234
that has someone
different on the cover.

00:09:58.234 --> 00:10:01.634
And I have the
first copy of it right here.

00:10:01.634 --> 00:10:04.067
That is Derek Jeter
right there on the cover.

00:10:04.067 --> 00:10:05.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:05.267 --> 00:10:08.400
Come on, bud.
That's what I'm talking about.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:10.901
Back on the cover of the game!

00:10:10.901 --> 00:10:14.634
-Thank you.
Thank you, thank you. Thank you.

00:10:14.634 --> 00:10:17.801
You know,
I've been contemplating

00:10:17.801 --> 00:10:21.033
coming out of retirement
for a few years.

00:10:21.033 --> 00:10:22.467
-We're waiting for
this moment right here.

00:10:22.467 --> 00:10:23.934
Do you have an announcement?

00:10:23.934 --> 00:10:26.968
-Yeah, I figured it's a lot
easier to do it in a video game

00:10:26.968 --> 00:10:28.367
than it actually is to do
in real life.

00:10:28.367 --> 00:10:29.501
-I mean, come on.

00:10:29.501 --> 00:10:32.133
That's got to --
Do you remember playing yourself

00:10:32.133 --> 00:10:33.267
for the first time
in a video game?

00:10:33.267 --> 00:10:34.334
-I do. Yeah, I do.

00:10:34.334 --> 00:10:35.467
And when you play
the first time,

00:10:35.467 --> 00:10:38.868
first of all,
it's an awesome experience

00:10:38.868 --> 00:10:40.367
playing as yourself,
'cause you grew up

00:10:40.367 --> 00:10:42.100
and you're playing
as your favorite players,

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.834
but then, you know,
my mind just went to,

00:10:43.834 --> 00:10:47.300
"How can I rig it so,
actually, that my performance

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.868
is better in the video game than
it actually is on the field?"

00:10:49.868 --> 00:10:52.667
I didn't figure that out,
but that's my first thought.

00:10:52.667 --> 00:10:55.000
-Yeah, you can't do --
Yeah, they actually --

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:57.467
I wanted to see what
I would look like if I was

00:10:57.467 --> 00:11:01.200
in the video game, and our
friends at "MLB The Show"

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:02.334
mocked this up.

00:11:02.334 --> 00:11:04.300
What do you think of this guy?
-Wow.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:06.400
-Look at this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.901
-Wow. Well, I would --

00:11:09.901 --> 00:11:13.033
-The guns are --
-Yeah, I would first -- Yeah.

00:11:13.033 --> 00:11:14.400
That's what I'd point out.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:18.801
The arms are not really
proportionate or, you know --

00:11:18.801 --> 00:11:21.400
-It's fantastic.
That looks exactly like me.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:28.000
-And I've never seen you look
serious, so this is another one.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.634
So, have you played
baseball before?

00:11:30.634 --> 00:11:32.567
-Yeah. I'm very athletic.
-Yeah.

00:11:32.567 --> 00:11:33.667
-Yeah, I'm athletic.
I've played baseball.

00:11:33.667 --> 00:11:34.901
I've played golf.

00:11:34.901 --> 00:11:36.767
-We're going to get you
some Greatness Wins gear.

00:11:36.767 --> 00:11:38.434
There you go.
-Will you get me some?

00:11:38.434 --> 00:11:42.634
-I will, I will so you can
look athletic and --

00:11:42.634 --> 00:11:44.734
-Look athletic? I am athletic.

00:11:44.734 --> 00:11:47.767
I've won golf tournaments.

00:11:47.767 --> 00:11:49.467
It's very famous.

00:11:49.467 --> 00:11:51.801
We've all seen Derek Jeter,

00:11:51.801 --> 00:11:54.300
what a Derek Jeter
home run has looked like,

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.067
but there's something
we haven't seen.

00:11:56.067 --> 00:11:58.367
Let's take a look at this.

00:11:58.367 --> 00:12:03.934
-Now batting, number 24,
Jimmy Fallon.

00:12:03.934 --> 00:12:05.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.434
Come on, buddy! Focus up.

00:12:07.434 --> 00:12:10.267
Yes! Oh, my!

00:12:10.267 --> 00:12:11.801
Look at that shot.

00:12:11.801 --> 00:12:13.234
-If you are actually going --

00:12:13.234 --> 00:12:15.834
If you're actually going to
make this realistic,

00:12:15.834 --> 00:12:18.501
they should have you run around
the bases the other way.

00:12:18.501 --> 00:12:20.400
I think that would work.
That would work for you.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:21.634
-Congratulations
on everything --

00:12:21.634 --> 00:12:25.133
the kids, the cover,
Turn 2, by the way.

00:12:25.133 --> 00:12:27.634
Come back whenever you want.
Please, next week, come back.

00:12:27.634 --> 00:12:29.167
Derek Jeter, everybody.

00:12:29.167 --> 00:12:32.133
"MLB The Show's"
"Collector Edition"

00:12:32.133 --> 00:12:34.634
is available starting
March 24th.

00:12:34.634 --> 00:12:36.634
We'll be right back with
Rita Ora.

